  "sourceNotes": "Imperial biscuits are a fixture of Scottish baking, particularly in the central belt and Borders regions. They appear in Scottish community cookery books from the early 20th century and are documented in references including F. Marian McNeill's The Scots Kitchen (1929). The name 'imperial' suggests their formality as a teatime biscuit; they are also known in some areas as 'Belgium biscuits' or 'German biscuits', names whose origins are now obscure.",
